response:
[' ```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mtx; // Thread safety mutex

void insertAfter(std::vector<std::pair<std::string, int>>& vec, const std::pair<std::string, int>& keyToFind, const std::pair<std::string, int>& newPair) {
    auto it = std::find_if(vec.begin(), vec.end(), [&](const std::pair<std::string, int>& pair) { return pair.first == keyToFind.first; });

    if (it == vec.end()) {
        vec.push_back(newPair);
    } else {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
        it = vec.insert(++it, newPair); // Increment past the found position to insert after it
        lock.unlock();
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a mutex for thread safety and handles edge cases where the first key is not found in the vector by appending the new pair at the end of the vector. The `std::find_if()` function is used to find the first occurrence of the key, and `std::vector::insert()` function is used to efficiently insert the new pair after the found position.

To handle large datasets more efficiently, this function uses a linear search instead of a binary search (which requires sorted data) to find the key in the vector, making it suitable for unsorted vectors. However, if the vector is frequently accessed and modified concurrently by multiple threads, consider using a data structure such as a balanced binary tree or a hash table to improve lookup time while maintaining thread safety.

This implementation assumes that the larger system includes proper database access functions for dynamic data updates and logging functionality for audit purposes. Keep in mind that any additional dependencies should be accounted for when implementing this function within your system.']

