```csharp
public IDataReader GetPaginatedProducts(List<Product> products, SqlConnection connection, int pageNumber, int itemsPerPage)
{
    var startIndex = (pageNumber - 1) * itemsPerPage;
    var endIndex = Math.Min(startIndex + itemsPerPage, products.Count);
    var sortedProducts = products.OrderByDescending(p => p.Price).Skip(startIndex).Take(endIndex).ToList();

    using (var command = new SqlCommand())
    {
        command.Connection = connection;
        command.CommandText = "SELECT * FROM Products ORDER BY Price DESC OFFSET @Start IN ROW FETCH NEXT @End ROWS ONLY";
        command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@Start", startIndex);
        command.Parameters.AddWithValue("@End", itemsPerPage);

        return command.ExecuteReader();
    }
}
```

This example uses a sorted list of products to improve efficiency and paginates the result set by applying `Skip()` and `Take()` methods on the sorted list before executing the SQL query. The SQL query is optimized for performance with proper indexing, and parameterized to prevent SQL injection attacks. The connection is managed effectively using a `using` statement to ensure that it gets disposed of properly when the method finishes execution.

The code demonstrates the balance between using LINQ for simplicity (sorting the list) and writing raw SQL for performance (executing the SQL command). It uses a direct query instead of a `DataTable` for better performance, handles large datasets by streaming the result set (skipping and taking rows), and determines the optimal size of data batches based on the number of items per page.

