The tradeoffs considered here include using built-in functions for reading files and counting lines, as well as implementing POSIX threads for managing the multi-threaded environment. The number of threads is dynamically adjusted based on the system's available resources (e.g., CPU cores). Memory management is optimized to avoid leaks or excessive memory usage when reading large files. Proper error handling across multiple threads without deadlocks or race conditions is ensured using pthread functions like `pthread_create` and `pthread_join`.']

