[' ```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    int value;
} Record;

long offsets[10000];
int keys[10000];
Record records[10000];

pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

int init_data(int size) {
    if (size < 1 || size > 10000) {
        return -1;
    }

    // Allocate memory for offsets, keys and records arrays, ensuring size <= 10000
    long *temp_offsets = malloc(sizeof(long) * size);
    if (!temp_offsets) {
        free(offsets);
        return -1;
    }
    int *temp_keys = malloc(sizeof(int) * size);
    if (!temp_keys) {
        free(temp_offsets);
        free(offsets);
        return -1;
    }
    Record *temp_records = malloc(sizeof(Record) * size);
    if (!temp_records) {
        free(temp_offsets);
        free(temp_keys);
        free(offsets);
        return -1;
    }

    // Copy data to allocated arrays and lock the mutex to ensure thread safety
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
    memcpy(offsets, temp_offsets, sizeof(long) * size);
    memcpy(keys, temp_keys, sizeof(int) * size);
    memcpy(records, temp_records, sizeof(Record) * size);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    // Free temporary arrays and return success code
    free(temp_offsets);
    free(temp_keys);
    free(temp_records);

    return 0;
}
```

This solution efficiently manages memory by dynamically allocating memory for the required arrays using malloc() function. The thread safety is ensured by using a mutex lock to protect the critical section of copying data from temporary arrays to main arrays, preventing race conditions when multiple threads call this function concurrently. Memory usage should be optimized as the allocated memory is released immediately after copying it to the main arrays, avoiding excessive allocation or fragmentation.

By using a pre-initialized mutex, the overhead of initializing a mutex before every call to `init_data()` is eliminated, improving performance in a multi-threaded environment. The function handles invalid input gracefully by returning an appropriate error code if the size parameter is out of bounds (1 to 10,000 inclusive). If any allocation fails, deallocate all previously allocated memory and return -1.']

