```javascript
const asyncReduce = (arr, initialValue, callback) => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        let accumulatedValue = initialValue;
        const reducer = (accumulator, currentValue, index, array) => {
            callback(accumulator, currentValue, index, array)
                .then(result => {
                    accumulatedValue = result;
                    if (array.length - 1 === index) {
                        resolve(accumulatedValue);
                    } else {
                        reducer(accumulatedValue, array[index + 1], index + 1, array);
                    }
                })
                .catch(error => reject(error));
        };
        reducer(accumulatedValue, arr[0], 0, arr);
    });
};
```

Both functions are designed to be efficient in terms of memory usage, handle large datasets, and manage errors gracefully. The C function uses dynamic allocation for the record array and ensures proper memory deallocation through `free()`. The JavaScript function handles asynchronous operations using Promises and logs information to the console."]

