The function handles memory allocation errors gracefully by checking if the file can be opened, and freeing the allocated memory for a record when it encounters an invalid or missing field in the CSV file. It also manages the maximum number of records that can be stored using max_records parameter to handle potential overflow conditions.

The function is efficient both in terms of time complexity (O(n)), as it reads and processes the CSV file line by line, and space complexity (O(1)) for the average case, as it only stores a single line at a time. It minimizes I/O operations by using buffered I/O (fgets) to read the data from the file in chunks.

The implementation is flexible and scalable, allowing it to process different CSV files with varying numbers of records. The function validates the data in the CSV file before populating the structures to ensure data integrity.']

